Superficial partial-thickness burn from touching motorcycle exhaust to medial calf
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Tense clear blisters, Exquisite sensitivity to touch, Hot metal contact mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severely painful burn with rapid blister formation requiring urgent assessment for depth, debridement, and specialized burn dressing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a direct contact burn with a high-temperature motorcycle exhaust, causing immediate severe pain and blistering. Urgent clinical evaluation was required to definitively assess burn depth, rule out full-thickness tissue necrosis, and apply immediate occlusive burn dressings to prevent severe infection and manage uncontrolled pain prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I accidentally backed my leg into a hot motorcycle exhaust pipe, and it has blistered up and is burning intensely.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of momentary accidental physical contact with a highly heated metallic surface occurring 45 minutes ago. This resulted in a sharply demarcated area of painful erythema conforming precisely to the shape of the offending hot object on the medial calf. There was rapid development of thin-walled tense blisters containing clear serous fluid. The patient reports exquisite tenderness to ambient air currents, light touch, and minor temperature changes. The intense pain is constant and unrelieved by simple positioning.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but with a deliberate limp to avoid stretching the burned skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor spatial awareness issue leading to injury; advised caution around heated machinery.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate acute pain, visibly guarding the affected lower leg.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals a 5x10cm area of tense clear blisters on the medial calf. The burn demonstrates exquisite sensitivity to touch and ambient air. There is a surrounding border of intensely blanching skin erythema. The skin underlying any spontaneously ruptured blisters appears pink, moist, and glistening. Intact capillary refill within the affected dermal layer indicates complete preservation of dermal vascularity. There is a total absence of any white, leathery, or charred insensate tissue indicative of full-thickness destruction. Normal distal circulation and neurological sensation in the affected lower extremity.
Environmental Observations: Motorcycle parked outside; engine cool.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Applied running cold water for 15 minutes prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial partial-thickness burn to medial calf - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Full-thickness (3rd degree) burn - Ruled out due to: Exquisite pain sensitivity (intact nerves), brisk capillary refill, and absence of white/leathery insensate eschar.

Deep partial-thickness burn - Ruled out due to: Bright pink, glistening dermis rather than a mottled, sluggishly blanching pale wound bed.

Contact dermatitis - Ruled out due to: Immediate acute thermal mechanism and instantaneous blister formation rather than a delayed allergic response.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Direct contact with high-temperature engine components requires precise clinical estimation of burn depth to guide management. Bedside evaluation successfully confirmed a superficial partial-thickness burn with entirely preserved deep dermal perfusion and sensation. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to perform immediate, sterile dressing application using specialized hydrogel or silver-based products, which instantly halts the severe neuropathic pain cycle and prevents deep secondary infection, avoiding an unnecessary presentation to a specialized emergency burn unit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Debrided spontaneously ruptured blister skin using sterile technique. Intact blisters were left undisturbed to act as a natural biological dressing. Applied a specialized burn dressing (e.g., Acticoat or hydrogel sheet) covered with a secondary absorbent layer and light compression.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the burn dressing entirely sealed and dry for 3 to 5 days. Undisturbed specialized burn dressings maintain a perfect moisture balance, which stops the raw nerve endings from firing and drastically reduces the chance of thick, restrictive scar tissue forming.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Specialized burn dressings (hydrogel/silver-impregnated).
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for burn wound inspection and dressing change.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the leg becomes massively swollen, if red streaks travel up the calf towards the groin, or if you develop a fever, chills, and increasing pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Burns are dynamic injuries; the depth can occasionally "declare" itself and appear deeper over the first 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The severe burning will subside dramatically within hours under the dressing. Blisters will reabsorb or pop naturally. Complete epithelialization typically takes 10 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if thick, green, foul-smelling fluid starts leaking from the bandage, or if the skin around the burn becomes hot, hard, and intensely red.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specialized dressing cools the tissue, prevents bacteria from entering the raw skin, and shields the sensitive nerve endings from moving air, providing immediate pain relief.
Actionable Steps: Do not burst intact blisters yourself. Keep the leg elevated on a stool when sitting to reduce throbbing. Take scheduled paracetamol for the first 48 hours.

